00:15:15.940 --> 00:15:19.150
The node pools are kind
of the pools of VMs

00:15:19.150 --> 00:15:22.520
that you want used
for your workload.

00:15:22.520 --> 00:15:31.520
So here I created a node pool
with 96 CPUs and 624 GB RAM.

00:15:31.520 --> 00:15:34.030
So this node pool, I
set it to auto-scale.

00:15:34.030 --> 00:15:37.510
So the minimum size is zero
and the maximum size is ten.

00:15:37.510 --> 00:15:40.390
So if there is no load on
the cluster, it auto scales

00:15:40.390 --> 00:15:44.000
that to zero, so you
don't pay for it.

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:47.380
So since I executed this job,
it will create an instance

00:15:47.380 --> 00:15:50.110
and start that instance
and run this workload.

00:15:50.110 --> 00:15:53.970
So it will take probably like
three or four minutes' time.

00:15:53.970 --> 00:15:55.990
So here are some of
the important concepts

00:15:55.990 --> 00:15:57.160
that we covered, right?

00:15:57.160 --> 00:16:01.750
The entry point is how you
want to specify your logic.

00:16:01.750 --> 00:16:05.520
You can specify your
logic as a Python file

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:07.820
or a Python function or class.

00:16:07.820 --> 00:16:13.200
We will-- I will show you the
Python class example later.

00:16:13.200 --> 00:16:16.070
Or you want to execute
a complete notebook,

00:16:16.070 --> 00:16:18.230
you can execute it.

00:16:18.230 --> 00:16:22.720
And when it comes to Python
dependency management, we can--

00:16:22.720 --> 00:16:27.460
either you can provide a base
Docker image, which is just

00:16:27.460 --> 00:16:28.970
very, very flexible, right?

00:16:28.970 --> 00:16:31.180
You can install not
only Python dependencies

00:16:31.180 --> 00:16:34.900
but also you can install
system dependencies like Cura

00:16:34.900 --> 00:16:38.280
or other system components.

00:16:38.280 --> 00:16:41.710
And if you don't want
to deal with the Docker,

00:16:41.710 --> 00:16:43.870
you can just give a
requirements.txt file

00:16:43.870 --> 00:16:47.790
and we will kind of
build Docker ourself.

00:16:47.790 --> 00:16:50.710
And right now we have
two types of jobs.

00:16:50.710 --> 00:16:51.990
One is called training.

00:16:51.990 --> 00:16:54.180
The other one is
online prediction.

00:16:54.180 --> 00:16:58.860
The training is very
simple, just takes your code

00:16:58.860 --> 00:17:00.370
and run it.

00:17:00.370 --> 00:17:03.150
So we have a support for
distributor training also.

00:17:03.150 --> 00:17:05.380
I will show that.

00:17:05.380 --> 00:17:08.640
And for the online prediction
is where you train your model

00:17:08.640 --> 00:17:12.900
and you wanted to kind of
put that into a service

00:17:12.900 --> 00:17:17.050
where you can make a HTTP
call and get back the results.

00:17:17.050 --> 00:17:21.369
So this is useful for
productionalizing your model.

00:17:21.369 --> 00:17:23.760
So once you have the
training of the model,

00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:29.880
if you want users of your app
or users of your internal users,

00:17:29.880 --> 00:17:34.390
they can kind of hit
this HTTP and point.

00:17:34.390 --> 00:17:37.290
Then lastly, I will kind
of dive into the back ends.

00:17:37.290 --> 00:17:39.660
I kind of keep that for now.

00:17:39.660 --> 00:17:43.055
We'll visit that later.

00:17:43.055 --> 00:17:47.250
So let's see what's
happening to the node.

00:17:47.250 --> 00:17:49.150
So still waiting.

00:17:49.150 --> 00:17:50.130
If I can refresh--

00:17:57.550 --> 00:18:00.370
So here, I can see
this, one node started.

00:18:03.170 --> 00:18:05.030
So where is that?

00:18:05.030 --> 00:18:07.980
So it's still not there yet.

00:18:07.980 --> 00:18:09.200
But that's fine.

00:18:09.200 --> 00:18:11.900
It will come back later.

00:18:11.900 --> 00:18:14.020
So diving into kind
of the architecture

00:18:14.020 --> 00:18:16.740
and the implementation.

00:18:16.740 --> 00:18:21.100
So we have three
distinct processes

00:18:21.100 --> 00:18:24.330
that we go through
whenever we schedule a job.

00:18:24.330 --> 00:18:27.880
So the first process
is the preprocessor.

00:18:27.880 --> 00:18:33.730
And this is how you specify
your code gets packaged.

00:18:33.730 --> 00:18:36.095
So either you can specify
it as a notebook, function,

00:18:36.095 --> 00:18:37.130
or Python.

00:18:37.130 --> 00:18:40.720
So the preprocessor
handles that logic.

00:18:40.720 --> 00:18:44.050
That second step is
the builder phase.

00:18:44.050 --> 00:18:48.620
And this is where we build
a Docker image out of that.

00:18:48.620 --> 00:18:53.092
And here we are supporting
multiple environments

00:18:53.092 --> 00:18:53.800
where you are in.

00:18:53.800 --> 00:18:54.310
Right?

00:18:54.310 --> 00:18:58.720
So, for example, you can
be in an environment lab,

00:18:58.720 --> 00:19:03.130
like a collab kind of online
hosted notebook environment.

00:19:03.130 --> 00:19:06.100
Or you are running
your notebook locally.

00:19:06.100 --> 00:19:09.810
Or you are running your notebook
in a Kubernetes cluster itself.

00:19:09.810 --> 00:19:13.270
So for different environments
we need different types

00:19:13.270 --> 00:19:14.360
of Docker builders.

00:19:14.360 --> 00:19:15.310
So that's why, OK.

00:19:15.310 --> 00:19:17.680
We have a couple of
varieties of Docker builders,

00:19:17.680 --> 00:19:20.950
not only just a local Docker
builder, but also remote.

00:19:20.950 --> 00:19:24.580
And we'll probably add some
Cloud Build support also.

00:19:24.580 --> 00:19:27.770
So in the deployer phases
where we built the Docker

00:19:27.770 --> 00:19:32.320
image of your code, and
we wanted to deploy it

00:19:32.320 --> 00:19:35.140
into kind of a cluster.

00:19:35.140 --> 00:19:39.220
And the job type matters.

00:19:39.220 --> 00:19:43.090
For example, if it's a
distributed TensorFlow job,

00:19:43.090 --> 00:19:46.990
we wanted to use a TF job
that's part of the Kubeflow.

00:19:46.990 --> 00:19:49.840
Or if you want to do
an online prediction,

00:19:49.840 --> 00:19:54.910
we wanted to do
serving a type of job.

00:19:54.910 --> 00:19:59.890
And one of the aims of
this Kubeflow [INAUDIBLE]

00:19:59.890 --> 00:20:03.710
is that we wanted to be
very modular and extensible.

00:20:03.710 --> 00:20:07.430
So we expose all these
APIs to the end user.

00:20:07.430 --> 00:20:10.780
So if you wanted to
build a different type

00:20:10.780 --> 00:20:13.210
of preprocessor-- for
example, let's say

00:20:13.210 --> 00:20:15.070
you wanted to kind
of take a notebook,

00:20:15.070 --> 00:20:18.340
and convert that into a
Python file and run that,

00:20:18.340 --> 00:20:20.470
you will be able to do that.

00:20:20.470 --> 00:20:22.870
And also, for example,
if your company

00:20:22.870 --> 00:20:26.260
has a different way of
building Docker containers,

00:20:26.260 --> 00:20:30.170
you can kind of plug
that inside of that.

00:20:30.170 --> 00:20:32.530
And on top of
that, what I showed

00:20:32.530 --> 00:20:35.180
is kind of the trained
Java API, which

00:20:35.180 --> 00:20:37.550
tries to hide all
these [INAUDIBLE],,

00:20:37.550 --> 00:20:41.500
and give you a very
simplified API where you just

00:20:41.500 --> 00:20:44.410
give your Python file
or your Python function.

00:20:44.410 --> 00:20:47.130
It goes and executes.

00:20:47.130 --> 00:20:53.330
So one of the most that we
have is the XGBoost training

00:20:53.330 --> 00:20:54.900
and deployment.

00:20:54.900 --> 00:21:00.780
So here, we have an
XGBoost training.

00:21:00.780 --> 00:21:03.110
So all these cells are
pretty standard cells.

00:21:03.110 --> 00:21:07.520
Like here I am kind of
reading a sample data set

00:21:07.520 --> 00:21:12.380
and doing some mutation,
like kind of replacing nulls

00:21:12.380 --> 00:21:15.600
with some mean values.

00:21:15.600 --> 00:21:20.343
And here I am doing
a very simple fit.

00:21:20.343 --> 00:21:21.510
This is nothing interesting.

00:21:21.510 --> 00:21:27.380
It's just trying to showcase
how you do XGBoost training.

00:21:27.380 --> 00:21:30.830
And both the eval and
save are pretty simple.

00:21:30.830 --> 00:21:32.360
I'm saving to our local file.

00:21:36.460 --> 00:21:39.400
And one more thing
that we are exposing

00:21:39.400 --> 00:21:42.920
is kind of a Python
class interface,

00:21:42.920 --> 00:21:46.920
where if you wanted to do
both training and prediction--

00:21:46.920 --> 00:21:49.050
so you can follow
this interface where

00:21:49.050 --> 00:21:52.180
you have to have a train
method and a predict method.

00:21:52.180 --> 00:21:55.700
First, let's look at the train
method and see what it does.

00:21:55.700 --> 00:21:58.690
So the train method
reads the input,

00:21:58.690 --> 00:22:02.590
converts that into NumPy
arrays, and trains that model

00:22:02.590 --> 00:22:06.190
with the NumPy arrays,
and does eval and save.

00:22:06.190 --> 00:22:10.340
And in the predict method,
ignore these feature names

00:22:10.340 --> 00:22:11.960
for a moment.

00:22:11.960 --> 00:22:14.380
Those are kind of
extra things that

00:22:14.380 --> 00:22:18.370
may not be applicable here.

00:22:18.370 --> 00:22:22.210
The main one is that you get
kind of a NumPy array input.

00:22:22.210 --> 00:22:24.630
And you're loading
the model that you

00:22:24.630 --> 00:22:26.080
saved from the training.

00:22:26.080 --> 00:22:29.110
And you are doing prediction
on that NumPy input,

00:22:29.110 --> 00:22:31.240
and returning the prediction.

00:22:31.240 --> 00:22:35.260
So here I've just written
in the same prediction,

00:22:35.260 --> 00:22:38.860
just for example.

00:22:38.860 --> 00:22:43.540
So if I wanted to kind of run
this training job locally,

00:22:43.540 --> 00:22:46.220
I will just say, house
train dot trained.

00:22:46.220 --> 00:22:46.720
Right?

00:22:46.720 --> 00:22:48.570
House serve dot train.

00:22:48.570 --> 00:22:50.890
Here it's a pretty simple model.

00:22:50.890 --> 00:22:53.070
So it executes pretty fast.

00:22:53.070 --> 00:22:55.020
So typically, our
data scientists,

00:22:55.020 --> 00:22:56.730
they wanted to kind
of train locally

00:22:56.730 --> 00:23:00.860
with a smaller data set before
training with a bigger data

00:23:00.860 --> 00:23:06.090
set so that their iterations
speed is much faster.

00:23:06.090 --> 00:23:07.620
Right?

00:23:07.620 --> 00:23:09.660
So here I did that.

00:23:09.660 --> 00:23:12.090
I trained locally.

00:23:12.090 --> 00:23:13.210
It works.

00:23:13.210 --> 00:23:15.810
So now, if I wanted
to kind of do

00:23:15.810 --> 00:23:18.840
the same thing on
a remote cluster,

00:23:18.840 --> 00:23:26.730
I do some configurations where
I have to store my output Docker

00:23:26.730 --> 00:23:31.510
files, and also kind of
creating the Python base image.

00:23:31.510 --> 00:23:32.850
So all these are defaults.

00:23:32.850 --> 00:23:35.470
I'm just showing it.

00:23:35.470 --> 00:23:37.050
But you don't have
to specify this.

00:23:37.050 --> 00:23:41.460
It will infer that
automatically.

00:23:41.460 --> 00:23:44.650
So we are going back
to the train job API.

00:23:44.650 --> 00:23:47.440
So before we gave
a Python function.

00:23:47.440 --> 00:23:50.550
So here we are giving
a Python class.

00:23:50.550 --> 00:23:53.250
So the train job will
take the Python class

00:23:53.250 --> 00:23:56.510
and package that
into a Docker image,

00:23:56.510 --> 00:23:59.470
and execute it in
the remote cluster.

00:23:59.470 --> 00:24:04.640
So you would a see
similar experience.

00:24:04.640 --> 00:24:07.580
So here we are also giving
it requirements.txt.

00:24:07.580 --> 00:24:09.490
So this one contains
all the Panda,

00:24:09.490 --> 00:24:11.920
scikit-learn, all
your requirements.

00:24:11.920 --> 00:24:15.790
So it's going to install all
those requirements for you

00:24:15.790 --> 00:24:17.410
in the Docker image.

00:24:17.410 --> 00:24:20.840
So it takes some
time to install that.

00:24:20.840 --> 00:24:22.960
So once it's
installed, it's going

00:24:22.960 --> 00:24:29.760
to push that container to
the remote Docker registry.

00:24:29.760 --> 00:24:32.490
So let it go.

00:24:32.490 --> 00:24:37.030
So in terms of
frameworks right now

00:24:37.030 --> 00:24:40.420
we are supporting
XGBoost and Lite GBM.

00:24:40.420 --> 00:24:44.130
And we have support for
TensorFlow in a single note

00:24:44.130 --> 00:24:45.520
PyTorch.

00:24:45.520 --> 00:24:47.620
Just a show of hands,
how many people

00:24:47.620 --> 00:24:52.240
here use XGBoost or
GBM for their work?

00:24:52.240 --> 00:24:53.380
More like tabular data.

00:24:53.380 --> 00:24:53.880
Right?

00:24:56.760 --> 00:24:58.950
And one more thing I
wanted to kind of hear

00:24:58.950 --> 00:25:00.040
is kind of the feedback.

00:25:00.040 --> 00:25:02.350
Because we are doing
this in open source.

00:25:02.350 --> 00:25:04.060
And we wanted to
kind of make sure

00:25:04.060 --> 00:25:07.420
that it's useful to a
wide range of audience,

00:25:07.420 --> 00:25:10.210
and not biased by either
internal Google use

00:25:10.210 --> 00:25:13.430
cases or our customers.

00:25:13.430 --> 00:25:15.250
So one of the things
that I'll show

00:25:15.250 --> 00:25:20.050
is kind of a distributed
support for Lite GBM.

00:25:20.050 --> 00:25:23.458
For the people who raised their
hands, how many people have

00:25:23.458 --> 00:25:25.000
kind of a requirement
for distributed

00:25:25.000 --> 00:25:29.180
training for XGBoost or GBM?

00:25:29.180 --> 00:25:31.970
Or a single node training
