00:25:58.305 --> 00:26:01.150
XGBoost.

00:26:01.150 --> 00:26:01.665
Yeah.

00:26:01.665 --> 00:26:04.165
KARTHIK RAMASAMY: The problem
I can show you that are more--

00:26:04.165 --> 00:26:06.880
it should be simple enough
for you to give it a try.

00:26:11.720 --> 00:26:12.220
Yeah.

00:26:12.220 --> 00:26:15.730
So it's pushing the
Docker image here.

00:26:19.110 --> 00:26:23.040
So since I kind of
cleared out all my cache,

00:26:23.040 --> 00:26:24.540
it's rebuilding it.

00:26:24.540 --> 00:26:28.260
When you typically
do this second time,

00:26:28.260 --> 00:26:29.550
it should be really fast.

00:26:34.170 --> 00:26:35.430
So yeah.

00:26:35.430 --> 00:26:37.310
It did run.

00:26:37.310 --> 00:26:39.350
And it's very similar
to the local running.

00:26:39.350 --> 00:26:40.110
It's pretty fast.

00:26:40.110 --> 00:26:43.850
So there's not much time
to wait for it running.

00:26:46.520 --> 00:26:48.830
And the other one that
I wanted to kind of show

00:26:48.830 --> 00:26:50.600
is how to do our prediction.

00:26:50.600 --> 00:26:57.390
So going back to the
class that we had--

00:26:57.390 --> 00:26:58.430
so this predict method.

00:26:58.430 --> 00:26:59.030
Right?

00:26:59.030 --> 00:27:01.880
So this predict method,
you can call it locally.

00:27:01.880 --> 00:27:04.940
But if you wanted to kind
of put it as an HTTP server,

00:27:04.940 --> 00:27:08.750
it takes a lot of effort to kind
of package that and ship it.

00:27:08.750 --> 00:27:09.380
Right?

00:27:09.380 --> 00:27:13.360
So we wanted to kind of
simplify that process.

00:27:13.360 --> 00:27:16.740
So here, instead of
a train job, you say,

00:27:16.740 --> 00:27:19.590
I need a prediction end point.

00:27:19.590 --> 00:27:21.720
And you give this
similar arguments,

00:27:21.720 --> 00:27:26.370
like the Python input
class, your requirements.

00:27:26.370 --> 00:27:27.990
One more thing
here you are giving

00:27:27.990 --> 00:27:33.540
is the trained model from
your previous training.

00:27:33.540 --> 00:27:35.820
And that's it.

00:27:35.820 --> 00:27:38.100
So if I exclude
this, what it does

00:27:38.100 --> 00:27:42.890
is that first it packages
your Python class

00:27:42.890 --> 00:27:45.300
around an HTTP platform.

00:27:45.300 --> 00:27:48.210
So we use a library
called Seldon,

00:27:48.210 --> 00:27:52.380
which is another popular open
source framework for doing

00:27:52.380 --> 00:27:55.250
online prediction.

00:27:55.250 --> 00:27:58.070
So since I built the
Docker image previously,

00:27:58.070 --> 00:28:00.380
this thing came up pretty fast.

00:28:00.380 --> 00:28:03.530
So it's waiting for the
end point to be created.

00:28:03.530 --> 00:28:07.670
This will take probably 10 to
20 seconds for it to happen.

00:28:10.730 --> 00:28:13.360
So then once the end
point comes back--

00:28:13.360 --> 00:28:17.650
so you can take a NumPy
array and do a prediction

00:28:17.650 --> 00:28:18.700
against that end point.

00:28:22.630 --> 00:28:26.950
So let that happen.

00:28:26.950 --> 00:28:29.110
When talking about the
distributed support,

00:28:29.110 --> 00:28:33.100
we have a support for
a distributed training

00:28:33.100 --> 00:28:34.670
for Lite GBM.

00:28:34.670 --> 00:28:37.930
So yeah.

00:28:37.930 --> 00:28:43.330
So we got an end point where it
says kind of an IP address, end

00:28:43.330 --> 00:28:45.500
port, and a path.

00:28:45.500 --> 00:28:48.700
So if I go ahead and do
the end point prediction,

00:28:48.700 --> 00:28:53.590
it basically calls that numPy
array against that HTTP end

00:28:53.590 --> 00:28:56.570
point and gives
back the results.

00:28:56.570 --> 00:28:58.110
So there will be more--

00:28:58.110 --> 00:29:01.000
we'll improve the API to
be more easier to use.

00:29:01.000 --> 00:29:03.790
Like probably giving
back Panda's data

00:29:03.790 --> 00:29:06.700
frame is a much better use case.

00:29:06.700 --> 00:29:09.890
And you can go ahead and delete
that end point if you want.

00:29:15.090 --> 00:29:15.590
Yeah.

00:29:18.242 --> 00:29:20.450
AUDIENCE: I'm just wondering,
this essentially acts--

