00:30:05.090 --> 00:30:08.310
It's not even ML related fun.

00:30:08.310 --> 00:30:12.030
The one thing that is ML
related and framework specific

00:30:12.030 --> 00:30:14.130
is the distributor
training part.

00:30:14.130 --> 00:30:16.290
Because for the
distributor training,

00:30:16.290 --> 00:30:20.280
each framework requires
kind of a specific setup.

00:30:20.280 --> 00:30:23.520
For example, if you are
doing a distributed training

00:30:23.520 --> 00:30:26.490
for TensorFlow, it needs
specific environment variables

00:30:26.490 --> 00:30:27.480
to be set up.

00:30:27.480 --> 00:30:30.480
If you're doing
for XGBoost, there

00:30:30.480 --> 00:30:32.080
are a couple of ways to do it.

00:30:32.080 --> 00:30:36.230
Some needs kind of MPA
systems to be set up.

00:30:36.230 --> 00:30:39.990
So we are adding
framework specific support

00:30:39.990 --> 00:30:42.760
for the distributed learning.

00:30:42.760 --> 00:30:44.160
But for single
node training, you

00:30:44.160 --> 00:30:46.860
should be able to use
any framework you want.

00:30:52.570 --> 00:30:55.510
The other one that
we are kind of--

00:30:55.510 --> 00:30:57.010
So what I showed
with the XGBoost

00:30:57.010 --> 00:30:59.270
is where it's a Python API.

00:30:59.270 --> 00:30:59.770
Right?

00:30:59.770 --> 00:31:05.080
So you are kind of using
the Python XGBoost API.

00:31:05.080 --> 00:31:11.160
For Lite GBM we
went ahead and did

00:31:11.160 --> 00:31:13.170
kind of a native
integration with the CLI

00:31:13.170 --> 00:31:14.610
version of the API.

00:31:14.610 --> 00:31:15.480
So I will show that.

00:31:18.690 --> 00:31:22.260
So a lot of times,
especially for Lite GBM,

00:31:22.260 --> 00:31:24.350
a lot of features that
are exposed as a CLI,

00:31:24.350 --> 00:31:26.410
that's very useful.

00:31:26.410 --> 00:31:31.800
So a lot of users, they want
to kind of use the CLI version.

00:31:31.800 --> 00:31:37.530
And here we did kind of a
very native integration, where

00:31:37.530 --> 00:31:39.520
so this is the config
that that you typically

00:31:39.520 --> 00:31:41.470
use to train a Lite GBM model.

00:31:41.470 --> 00:31:44.950
Like this is similar to
an XGBoost model thing.

00:31:44.950 --> 00:31:46.570
Right?

00:31:46.570 --> 00:31:49.660
Because unlike
TensorFlow models,

00:31:49.660 --> 00:31:51.680
the model architecture
is pretty fixed.

00:31:51.680 --> 00:31:54.820
So you control only
the hyper parameters.

00:31:54.820 --> 00:31:58.360
So here I am giving
all IP parameters,

00:31:58.360 --> 00:32:01.180
or configuration for the model.

00:32:01.180 --> 00:32:04.580
And here are the input
data and the output

00:32:04.580 --> 00:32:07.240
data, all specified
as a GCS path.

00:32:07.240 --> 00:32:11.560
So GCS is our Google
Cloud Storage.

00:32:11.560 --> 00:32:17.320
Kind of takes care of
all the storage for you.

00:32:17.320 --> 00:32:21.460
And the users just go ahead and
do a Lite GBM dot executable.

00:32:21.460 --> 00:32:23.620
Let me go through this,
and run this process.

00:32:26.337 --> 00:32:26.920
I'll show you.

00:32:26.920 --> 00:32:29.540
You don't have to do that
one, because I already

00:32:29.540 --> 00:32:31.630
built that container.

00:32:38.430 --> 00:32:44.250
So here it's much more native
integration towards Lite GBM,

00:32:44.250 --> 00:32:48.750
where you're not controlling--

00:32:48.750 --> 00:32:52.470
you don't have to build
any specific Python

00:32:52.470 --> 00:32:53.280
class or wrapper.

00:32:53.280 --> 00:32:55.230
You just give this
configuration file.

00:32:55.230 --> 00:32:58.200
And we are going to execute
Lite GBM with that configuration

00:32:58.200 --> 00:32:59.130
file.

00:32:59.130 --> 00:33:01.320
The one interesting
part is that in order

00:33:01.320 --> 00:33:05.160
to go from a single node to a
distributed node in Lite GBM,

00:33:05.160 --> 00:33:07.050
you just change one parameter.

00:33:07.050 --> 00:33:11.140
So I will show you the
distributed version of it.

00:33:11.140 --> 00:33:13.830
So here, the Lite GBM
itself has a parameter

00:33:13.830 --> 00:33:15.940
called number of machines.

00:33:15.940 --> 00:33:18.555
So you change that
to-- like here it's

00:33:18.555 --> 00:33:21.030
three out of whatever you want.

00:33:21.030 --> 00:33:27.570
And when you execute
that with that parameter,

00:33:27.570 --> 00:33:30.420
we allocate that many workers
and do the distributed

00:33:30.420 --> 00:33:31.380
training.

00:33:31.380 --> 00:33:35.360
So this is where doing kind
of the framework level support

00:33:35.360 --> 00:33:37.920
is very useful and very fast.

00:33:42.840 --> 00:33:46.800
And what we have seen is that,
and specifically why we chose

00:33:46.800 --> 00:33:51.030
Lite GBM specifically, is
that in our benchmarks,

00:33:51.030 --> 00:33:55.400
we scaled up to
terabytes of data.

00:33:55.400 --> 00:34:01.260
And it gave pretty fast results,
and closer to linear scaling.

00:34:06.310 --> 00:34:08.050
Let me [INAUDIBLE].

00:34:10.389 --> 00:34:15.090
So that's the light GBM one.

00:34:15.090 --> 00:34:16.110
Let me try to see.

00:34:31.800 --> 00:34:32.370
OK.

00:34:32.370 --> 00:34:36.960
So again, here you can
resource things pretty easily.

00:34:36.960 --> 00:34:41.670
You can say how many cores you
want, how much memory you want.

00:34:41.670 --> 00:34:45.389
And if you say a stream
log is equal to true,

00:34:45.389 --> 00:34:47.830
it will stream the logs.

00:34:47.830 --> 00:34:50.699
But if you say stream
log equal to false,

00:34:50.699 --> 00:34:54.210
you can probably easily put
a fault loop on top of this

00:34:54.210 --> 00:34:56.610
and do very basic
hyper parameter tuning.

00:34:56.610 --> 00:34:59.130
So if you have 10
hyper parameters,

00:34:59.130 --> 00:35:02.730
you can kind of go through
that pretty simple.

00:35:02.730 --> 00:35:07.020
And all the model outputs are
saved into a specific GCS path.

00:35:07.020 --> 00:35:13.360
So you can kind of go back
and [INAUDIBLE] that files.

00:35:13.360 --> 00:35:15.715
So let me see if I
can execute this part.

00:35:18.590 --> 00:35:22.990
So all of this are happening
with auto scale enabled.

00:35:22.990 --> 00:35:26.430
So if we go back to
that, the cluster

00:35:26.430 --> 00:35:30.140
scales automatically
whenever you execute a job.

00:35:30.140 --> 00:35:34.860
And it goes back when you don't.

00:35:34.860 --> 00:35:37.530
But this seems to
be the same problem.

00:35:37.530 --> 00:35:38.030
Right?

00:35:38.030 --> 00:35:40.550
So it's like my paths
are not scheduled.

00:35:40.550 --> 00:35:43.011
Probably I'm hitting
some limits or something.

00:35:52.150 --> 00:35:55.935
So let me show you another API.

00:36:02.740 --> 00:36:05.320
So similarly, we have
a separate support

00:36:05.320 --> 00:36:07.870
for TensorFlow distributed,
like if you want

00:36:07.870 --> 00:36:10.060
to do TensorFlow distributed.

00:36:10.060 --> 00:36:14.560
So here is an example
of a simple mnist

00:36:14.560 --> 00:36:17.980
TensorFlow based
on the TF 2.0 API.

00:36:17.980 --> 00:36:24.025
So here we are using Keras and
the new distribution strategy.

00:36:28.730 --> 00:36:33.330
And here I am using kind
of the lower level APIs

00:36:33.330 --> 00:36:36.660
that I talked about, just to
showcase how flexibility is.

00:36:36.660 --> 00:36:37.290
Right?

00:36:37.290 --> 00:36:41.070
So I am constructing
this preprocessor.

00:36:41.070 --> 00:36:43.800
And I would recommend
this lower level API

00:36:43.800 --> 00:36:46.530
for people who are very
familiar with Kubernetes,

00:36:46.530 --> 00:36:50.640
and they want to control kind
of each step of the process.

00:36:50.640 --> 00:36:54.120
So you can kind of do
the pre-processing first.

00:36:54.120 --> 00:36:56.370
Then you can choose what
builder you wanted to use,

00:36:56.370 --> 00:36:59.860
like if you want to use
a local Docker builder,

00:36:59.860 --> 00:37:00.810
you can use this.

00:37:03.540 --> 00:37:06.890
And so we have a TF Java
API, where you can say,

00:37:06.890 --> 00:37:10.190
I want the worker count three.

00:37:10.190 --> 00:37:12.410
I'm not using the parameter
server style training.

00:37:12.410 --> 00:37:15.860
So I'm just putting the
chief count as equal to 0.

00:37:15.860 --> 00:37:19.145
And if you do that--

00:37:19.145 --> 00:37:21.690
I think it's still running.

00:37:21.690 --> 00:37:22.190
No.

00:37:22.190 --> 00:37:23.480
This is completed.

00:37:23.480 --> 00:37:26.270
So basically, you can
do kind of a distributed

00:37:26.270 --> 00:37:29.435
training with this API.

00:37:33.040 --> 00:37:35.620
So we are adding more
framework level support.

00:37:35.620 --> 00:37:42.140
For example, we'll be adding
distributed PyTorch support,

00:37:42.140 --> 00:37:45.410
and also distributed
XGBoost support also.

00:37:45.410 --> 00:37:46.653
There's a question.

00:37:48.072 --> 00:37:50.437
AUDIENCE: If I want to use
[INAUDIBLE] for distributed

00:37:50.437 --> 00:37:52.802
training, can I
still use Kubeflow?

00:37:56.750 --> 00:37:57.980
KARTHIK RAMASAMY: Yeah.

00:37:57.980 --> 00:37:59.270
[INAUDIBLE]

00:37:59.270 --> 00:38:00.530
You can use it.

00:38:00.530 --> 00:38:06.350
It's a little bit tricky,
but you can do that.

00:38:06.350 --> 00:38:09.150
Like with those lower level
APIs, you can do that.

00:38:09.150 --> 00:38:11.030
There's nothing preventing you.

00:38:14.060 --> 00:38:15.060
Yes.

00:38:15.060 --> 00:38:15.962
Yeah.

00:38:15.962 --> 00:38:19.332
The auto-scaling,
everything was really done.

00:38:19.332 --> 00:38:21.540
AUDIENCE: My question is
about hyperparameter tuning.

00:38:21.540 --> 00:38:25.620
So I was wondering if you wanted
to integrate Raytune or Katib,

00:38:25.620 --> 00:38:28.915
would you do that at
a level of calling

00:38:28.915 --> 00:38:30.040
varying functions directly?

00:38:30.040 --> 00:38:34.502
Or would there be a train
job at the API level?

00:38:34.502 --> 00:38:35.460
KARTHIK RAMASAMY: Yeah.

00:38:35.460 --> 00:38:37.620
Rephrasing the
question, how do we

00:38:37.620 --> 00:38:40.350
go about hyperparameter tuning.

00:38:40.350 --> 00:38:42.150
And you mentioned about Katib.

00:38:42.150 --> 00:38:45.600
I don't know about the Raytune.

00:38:45.600 --> 00:38:46.360
I'm not familiar.

00:38:46.360 --> 00:38:48.140
Is it part of a bigger project?

00:38:48.140 --> 00:38:48.640
Or is it--

00:38:48.640 --> 00:38:49.930
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:38:49.930 --> 00:38:51.770
KARTHIK RAMASAMY: OK.

00:38:51.770 --> 00:38:52.270
Oh, yeah.

00:38:52.270 --> 00:38:52.770
Yeah.

00:38:52.770 --> 00:38:55.350
The Ray-- It's part of
the [INAUDIBLE] project.

00:38:55.350 --> 00:38:56.160
Right?

00:38:56.160 --> 00:39:01.370
So we are working on a better
integration with Katib.

00:39:01.370 --> 00:39:05.560
So, for example, if we
take the current API

00:39:05.560 --> 00:39:07.870
and write a for loop
around it, it works.

00:39:07.870 --> 00:39:10.580
But it launches all
the jobs at once.

00:39:10.580 --> 00:39:13.270
It doesn't kind of do
the Kube based training.

00:39:13.270 --> 00:39:15.990
Or if you want to
do early termination

00:39:15.990 --> 00:39:19.070
or do Bayesian optimization,
you can't do it.

00:39:19.070 --> 00:39:22.450
So we are in the process of
integrating that with Katib,

00:39:22.450 --> 00:39:25.257
since Katib was a
part of Kubeflow.

00:39:25.257 --> 00:39:26.465
we are planning to integrate.

00:39:26.465 --> 00:39:29.750
The API would probably
look like something

00:39:29.750 --> 00:39:33.310
that we'll keep
the train job API.

00:39:33.310 --> 00:39:37.060
But probably you are passing
in kind of a map of parameters

00:39:37.060 --> 00:39:38.830
that you want to sweep.

00:39:38.830 --> 00:39:42.270
So from your perspective,
we're just launching one job.

00:39:42.270 --> 00:39:45.460
But it will be translated
into multiple jobs.

00:39:45.460 --> 00:39:48.573
And the parallelism on how
many trials we want to execute

00:39:48.573 --> 00:39:49.740
will be controlled by Katib.

00:39:54.630 --> 00:39:56.280
AUDIENCE: I have a question.

00:39:56.280 --> 00:39:58.440
So Kubeflow and ML Engine.

00:39:58.440 --> 00:40:00.588
Are they supposed to
interchange themselves?

00:40:00.588 --> 00:40:02.130
Are they the same,
on the same level?

00:40:02.130 --> 00:40:05.360
Or how do they come together?

00:40:05.360 --> 00:40:11.920
KARTHIK RAMASAMY: So
rephrasing the question,

00:40:11.920 --> 00:40:15.600
there are two platforms, like
Kubeflow and ML Engine, which

00:40:15.600 --> 00:40:18.750
is our managed platform
that we recently

00:40:18.750 --> 00:40:21.420
rebranded as our AI platform.

00:40:21.420 --> 00:40:23.700
So how does that compare?

00:40:23.700 --> 00:40:26.790
I would say it's two
different offerings, based

00:40:26.790 --> 00:40:29.670
upon your requirement.

00:40:29.670 --> 00:40:32.460
For example, Kubeflow
is fully open source.

00:40:32.460 --> 00:40:34.810
Like Apache 2.0 license,
you can run it on prem.

00:40:34.810 --> 00:40:39.720
You can run it natively, but you
have to manage your Kubernetes

00:40:39.720 --> 00:40:41.340
cluster.

00:40:41.340 --> 00:40:43.950
GCP provides managed Kubernetes.

00:40:43.950 --> 00:40:46.080
But there's a lot
of things that you

00:40:46.080 --> 00:40:47.430
have to manage on top of that.

00:40:47.430 --> 00:40:48.330
Right?

00:40:48.330 --> 00:40:52.830
Like creating node pools,
creating your ingress erasers,

00:40:52.830 --> 00:40:54.310
things like that.

00:40:54.310 --> 00:40:58.860
So it's about whether you
wanted to kind of take

00:40:58.860 --> 00:41:01.470
care of all those
management by yourself,

00:41:01.470 --> 00:41:05.280
or you wanted to have a much
more cleaner API where you say,

00:41:05.280 --> 00:41:06.550
I just want to run this job.

00:41:06.550 --> 00:41:07.810
I don't care about cluster.

00:41:07.810 --> 00:41:10.110
Or I don't care about lifecycle.

00:41:10.110 --> 00:41:12.180
Just run this training job.

00:41:12.180 --> 00:41:14.010
And give back me output.

00:41:14.010 --> 00:41:17.220
So the AI platform
is much more tuned

00:41:17.220 --> 00:41:19.410
for that kind of a
managed offering.

00:41:25.340 --> 00:41:27.370
Yeah.

00:41:27.370 --> 00:41:33.370
From pricing point of view,
also, it's actually the same.

00:41:33.370 --> 00:41:36.570
Even in ML Engine, you
just pay for what you run.

00:41:36.570 --> 00:41:40.920
It's the GCP pricing,
the GC pricing.

00:41:40.920 --> 00:41:42.915
And so I think people prefer--

00:41:45.540 --> 00:41:51.330
what we have seen is that if you
need a very good customizable,

00:41:51.330 --> 00:41:55.620
like if you really want to
have access to your clusters,

00:41:55.620 --> 00:41:59.380
your nodes, probably Kubeflow
will be a better solution.

00:41:59.380 --> 00:42:03.770
But if you don't have a DevOps
team that can support you,

00:42:03.770 --> 00:42:05.985
ML Engine is a much
better solution.

00:42:08.540 --> 00:42:09.931
Any questions?

00:42:13.090 --> 00:42:15.360
Now, let me open this up.

00:42:36.890 --> 00:42:41.350
So since we talked
about Kubeflow,

00:42:41.350 --> 00:42:44.030
let me see the other one.

00:42:47.200 --> 00:42:47.700
Yeah.

00:42:47.700 --> 00:42:50.300
So this one ran.

00:42:50.300 --> 00:42:52.640
Remember the one
that we ran with kind

00:42:52.640 --> 00:42:55.230
of a 90 CPU and 600GB memory.

00:42:55.230 --> 00:42:57.020
So that ran in the printer.

00:42:57.020 --> 00:42:59.870
It has 96 CPU and 614.

00:42:59.870 --> 00:43:03.500
The reason why this is
96 and we asked for 90

00:43:03.500 --> 00:43:09.830
is that the node that it's
running, it has 96 CPUs.

00:43:09.830 --> 00:43:13.120
So it's printing that 96.

00:43:13.120 --> 00:43:17.150
Kubernetes usually schedules.

00:43:17.150 --> 00:43:20.010
But the node, it has
at least 90 CPUs.

00:43:20.010 --> 00:43:23.670
So this one has 96 CPUs.

00:43:23.670 --> 00:43:26.010
And if you wanted to kind
of do the same thing.

00:43:26.010 --> 00:43:26.510
Right?

00:43:26.510 --> 00:43:29.340
Like going back to
the simple example,

00:43:29.340 --> 00:43:34.160
if you wanted to do the
GPU one, like, again, this

00:43:34.160 --> 00:43:37.370
is very a little bit
Kubernetes specific syntax.

00:43:37.370 --> 00:43:40.580
We will probably have
a better API soon.

00:43:40.580 --> 00:43:49.230
So here I'm just going to print
out the GPU architecture here.

00:43:49.230 --> 00:43:51.920
So again, like if
I ask for a GPU,

00:43:51.920 --> 00:43:59.390
it kind of goes and spins up
the node and runs this process.

00:43:59.390 --> 00:44:04.160
But since I already am kind of
running this process before,

00:44:04.160 --> 00:44:05.420
the node is still alive.

00:44:05.420 --> 00:44:11.680
So kind of it prints the
B100 GPU that you have.

00:44:11.680 --> 00:44:15.870
So this is another example of
how you can optimize your cost

00:44:15.870 --> 00:44:16.370
structure.

00:44:16.370 --> 00:44:17.020
Right?

00:44:17.020 --> 00:44:21.350
So typically, if you wanted
to train a model of the GPU,

00:44:21.350 --> 00:44:23.840
you go and create a
VM with a GPU on it.

00:44:23.840 --> 00:44:26.870
You execute your model.

00:44:26.870 --> 00:44:29.180
But a lot of times you spent
on analyzing your model

00:44:29.180 --> 00:44:33.242
or looking at your data,
at the same time, the VM

00:44:33.242 --> 00:44:33.950
keeps on running.

00:44:33.950 --> 00:44:34.490
Right?

00:44:34.490 --> 00:44:36.890
That's a lot of money to pay.

00:44:36.890 --> 00:44:39.410
Typically, people shut
down the VM and kind

00:44:39.410 --> 00:44:42.350
of move the data out of
the VM to their local box,

00:44:42.350 --> 00:44:43.740
and analyze the data.

00:44:43.740 --> 00:44:49.760
But again, that's a lot of back
and forth data transferring

00:44:49.760 --> 00:44:52.620
and a lot of hassle.

00:44:52.620 --> 00:44:55.490
So with this SDK
what you can do is

00:44:55.490 --> 00:44:58.430
that you can kind of
create a very small VM

00:44:58.430 --> 00:45:01.190
instance with just a few CPUs.

00:45:01.190 --> 00:45:03.830
And you can do all your
analysis, model analysis,

00:45:03.830 --> 00:45:05.060
and visualizations.

00:45:05.060 --> 00:45:07.780
But when it comes to
actually training GPU,

00:45:07.780 --> 00:45:10.370
you can schedule it on
the remote cluster, which

00:45:10.370 --> 00:45:12.510
auto scales do your workflow.

00:45:12.510 --> 00:45:15.170
So essentially, you
are using the GPU nodes

00:45:15.170 --> 00:45:18.230
only when it's needed,
and shutting it down

00:45:18.230 --> 00:45:19.450
when you don't need it.

00:45:19.450 --> 00:45:23.040
And since it's bringing back the
results and the data for you,

00:45:23.040 --> 00:45:25.640
you don't have to manually
transfer any data sets.

00:45:30.030 --> 00:45:30.530
Yeah.

00:45:30.530 --> 00:45:32.510
This is like a similar
example where I

00:45:32.510 --> 00:45:36.250
run the mnist model on the GPU.

00:45:40.610 --> 00:45:41.700
Yeah.

00:45:41.700 --> 00:45:43.215
There's a question.

00:45:46.680 --> 00:45:51.030
So if you want to get
started, I would suggest

00:45:51.030 --> 00:45:53.650
going to the Kubeflow website.

00:45:53.650 --> 00:45:55.920
So we have very
good documentation

00:45:55.920 --> 00:45:57.370
on how to get started.

00:45:57.370 --> 00:46:03.420
So one of the ways, just a
GCP dock, AWS, an on prem

00:46:03.420 --> 00:46:05.320
even Microsoft dock also.

00:46:05.320 --> 00:46:10.060
So if you go to GCP,
we have a nice UI.

00:46:10.060 --> 00:46:10.560
Right?

00:46:10.560 --> 00:46:11.920
You can spin up a cluster.

00:46:11.920 --> 00:46:13.800
So if you go to
deploying using UI.

00:46:20.260 --> 00:46:22.840
So we have an app
to deploy Kubeflow.

00:46:22.840 --> 00:46:26.590
So that, you specify your
project name and a couple

00:46:26.590 --> 00:46:28.790
of other parameters.

00:46:28.790 --> 00:46:31.450
So you have documentation
on how to fill this form.

00:46:31.450 --> 00:46:33.370
And when you click
Create Deployment,

00:46:33.370 --> 00:46:35.770
it will create a
Kubernetes cluster for you

00:46:35.770 --> 00:46:38.870
and install Kubeflow
on top of that.

00:46:38.870 --> 00:46:43.340
Then you can go to
that Kubeflow cluster.

00:46:43.340 --> 00:46:47.360
You would probably get a URL at
the end of this installation.

00:46:47.360 --> 00:46:49.915
I'll go to my already
installed cluster.

00:46:58.140 --> 00:47:00.950
So this is the Kubeflow
dashboard you will get.

00:47:00.950 --> 00:47:05.090
So here, it has a couple of
components I will go through.

00:47:05.090 --> 00:47:06.690
One is the notebook component.

00:47:06.690 --> 00:47:11.680
So you can actually post
a notebook on the cluster.

00:47:11.680 --> 00:47:17.270
So you can say, I need a Jupyter
Notebook with this many CPUs.

00:47:17.270 --> 00:47:22.160
And you can specify
any GPUs you want, like

00:47:22.160 --> 00:47:25.790
with extra resources,
like Nvidia GPUs.

00:47:25.790 --> 00:47:27.980
And it will give
you kind of a link

00:47:27.980 --> 00:47:32.120
that you can use as
a hosted notebook.

00:47:32.120 --> 00:47:36.120
And also, this is the
Katlib that we talked about,

00:47:36.120 --> 00:47:43.180
which is a tool for doing
hyperparameter tuning.

00:47:43.180 --> 00:47:49.300
And here you can go
through creating a study.

00:47:49.300 --> 00:47:51.650
So a study is basically
multiple experiments

00:47:51.650 --> 00:47:56.810
that you do, and kind of find
out the best one you want.

00:47:56.810 --> 00:48:00.367
I'm having some hard time
loading the dashboard.

00:48:07.830 --> 00:48:08.360
Yep.

00:48:08.360 --> 00:48:12.120
So here, you can go and
kind of fill this form.

00:48:12.120 --> 00:48:15.000
So this is the current
API that we have.

00:48:15.000 --> 00:48:19.320
So again, this is not a very
efficient way of doing it.

00:48:19.320 --> 00:48:21.440
Like if you are doing
a lot of experiments,

00:48:21.440 --> 00:48:23.840
you don't want to go
and create it manually.

00:48:23.840 --> 00:48:27.770
But also, the other way is that
writing the cyaml file, again,

00:48:27.770 --> 00:48:30.330
it's a little bit
of a learning curve

00:48:30.330 --> 00:48:33.175
and not a very good interface
for data scientists.

00:48:33.175 --> 00:48:34.550
That's why we
wanted to integrate

00:48:34.550 --> 00:48:38.150
this API into the firing
API where you just

00:48:38.150 --> 00:48:39.230
give a Python map.

00:48:39.230 --> 00:48:42.816
And we go and search through
all those hyper parameters.

00:48:45.612 --> 00:48:50.340
Another big product that we
have are the Kubeflow Pipelines.

00:48:50.340 --> 00:48:55.790
So once you did all your
training and experimentation,

00:48:55.790 --> 00:48:58.770
now you wanted to put that
into a production pipeline,

00:48:58.770 --> 00:49:01.590
you can use this Kubeflow
Pipelines product.

00:49:01.590 --> 00:49:03.940
I'm not going to go
very deep into that.

00:49:03.940 --> 00:49:09.030
But generally explaining your
machine learning pipeline

00:49:09.030 --> 00:49:10.240
has multiple steps.

00:49:10.240 --> 00:49:13.950
So here we have a training,
prediction, model analysis,

00:49:13.950 --> 00:49:14.970
things like that.

00:49:14.970 --> 00:49:20.040
And you can create
production grade pipelines

00:49:20.040 --> 00:49:21.310
using this product.

00:49:21.310 --> 00:49:22.935
So Kubeflow is kind
of a big ecosystem.

00:49:22.935 --> 00:49:24.570
It has a lot of tools.

00:49:24.570 --> 00:49:26.830
And fairing is one of them.

00:49:26.830 --> 00:49:30.930
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:49:30.930 --> 00:49:32.100
KARTHIK RAMASAMY: Yeah.

00:49:32.100 --> 00:49:35.340
So there's a very strong
connection between TFX

00:49:35.340 --> 00:49:36.880
and Kubeflow Pipelines.

00:49:36.880 --> 00:49:40.590
So think of TFX is
a pipeline component

00:49:40.590 --> 00:49:44.190
for TensorFlow specific
things, and Kubeflow pipelines

00:49:44.190 --> 00:49:47.220
is for all frameworks.

00:49:47.220 --> 00:49:50.730
But we have very good support
of executing TFX Pipeline

00:49:50.730 --> 00:49:54.438
in Kubeflow Pipeline.

00:49:54.438 --> 00:49:55.230
Any other question?

00:49:55.230 --> 00:49:56.052
Yeah.

