then it get stopped clusters
and [INAUDIBLE] investigate,

00:54:26.530 --> 00:54:28.630
is that possible?

00:54:28.630 --> 00:54:32.480
KARTHIK RAMASAMY: So right now
you have to do it manually.

00:54:32.480 --> 00:54:34.920
There are a couple of profiling
information you can get.

00:54:34.920 --> 00:54:39.190
Like, for example, if you wanted
to get a model performance,

00:54:39.190 --> 00:54:42.460
that best tool would be
using a Tensor board.

00:54:42.460 --> 00:54:44.610
So you will right the
Tensor board logs.

00:54:44.610 --> 00:54:47.540
We are working on easy ways
to launch a Tensor board.

00:54:47.540 --> 00:54:49.592
Like one idea that
we have is that

00:54:49.592 --> 00:54:51.050
when you launch a
training job, you

00:54:51.050 --> 00:54:52.680
will get a URL for Tensor board.

00:54:52.680 --> 00:54:55.540
You can just go
there and see it.

00:54:55.540 --> 00:55:01.860
But right now, if you wanted
to monitor system metrics,

00:55:01.860 --> 00:55:05.310
if you go to the job name,
so usually that spits

00:55:05.310 --> 00:55:08.052
out a job name.

00:55:08.052 --> 00:55:09.760
Cause right now it
doesn't have the data,

00:55:09.760 --> 00:55:11.040
because the job is finished.

00:55:11.040 --> 00:55:14.160
But usually you will have CPU
disk kernel things for you

00:55:14.160 --> 00:55:15.440
to monitor.

00:55:15.440 --> 00:55:15.940
Yeah.

00:55:15.940 --> 00:55:17.732
AUDIENCE: But does
this also tell you

00:55:17.732 --> 00:55:19.080
the health of the [INAUDIBLE]?

00:55:19.080 --> 00:55:19.997
KARTHIK RAMASAMY: Yes.

00:55:19.997 --> 00:55:20.930
Everything.

00:55:20.930 --> 00:55:23.058
And the feature of
the Kubernetes itself,

00:55:23.058 --> 00:55:25.350
like if there is something
wrong with the [INAUDIBLE],,

00:55:25.350 --> 00:55:27.610
it will restart it.

00:55:27.610 --> 00:55:30.610
And going back to
the model monitoring,

00:55:30.610 --> 00:55:34.900
so right now you have to look at
the Tensor board and terminate.

00:55:34.900 --> 00:55:37.380
There's a setting
in TensorFlow itself

00:55:37.380 --> 00:55:40.950
to do some early termination.

00:55:40.950 --> 00:55:42.670
It's not very advanced.

00:55:42.670 --> 00:55:44.070
But you can do that.

00:55:52.710 --> 00:55:54.300
So that's a pretty good thing.

00:55:54.300 --> 00:55:58.730
I will kind of show you
another thing, touching

00:55:58.730 --> 00:56:00.970
upon this particular aspect.

00:56:00.970 --> 00:56:03.360
So you can actually run
a Tensor board instance

00:56:03.360 --> 00:56:06.220
and point that to a GCS bucket.

00:56:06.220 --> 00:56:08.760
And you can get all
your training blocks

00:56:08.760 --> 00:56:10.740
into the GCS bucket.

00:56:10.740 --> 00:56:14.107
So, for example, I will
show you a Tensor board

00:56:14.107 --> 00:56:14.940
that we are running.

00:56:38.490 --> 00:56:43.025
So that way, you should be
able to see multiple runs.

00:57:12.730 --> 00:57:13.870
I don't know.

00:57:13.870 --> 00:57:14.530
Some issue.

00:57:20.270 --> 00:57:23.170
But there's a feature
where in TensorFlow you

00:57:23.170 --> 00:57:24.640
take a [INAUDIBLE] bucket.

00:57:24.640 --> 00:57:28.685
And for every run, you log
into that particular bucket.

00:57:28.685 --> 00:57:30.310
So then in the Tensor
board, you should

00:57:30.310 --> 00:57:34.177
